<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How Many Rolls of Wallpaper Do I Need?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">These charts use standard rolls, 10 cm (4 in) of trim on every drop and the pattern repeat shown. They do not include the spare roll we recommend you add.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Walls 2.4 m high, 52 cm x 10.05 m rolls</th><th>Plain or no repeat</th><th>Large repeat (64 cm)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Feature wall 2.5 m wide</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Feature wall 3 m wide</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Feature wall 4 m wide</td><td>2</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Feature wall 5 m wide</td><td>3</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Room 3 x 3 m</td><td>6</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Room 3 x 4 m</td><td>7</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>Room 4 x 4 m</td><td>8</td><td>11</td></tr><tr><td>Room 4 x 5 m</td><td>9</td><td>12</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Walls 8 ft high, 20.5 in x 33 ft rolls</th><th>Plain or no repeat</th><th>Large repeat (25 in)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Feature wall 10 ft wide</td><td>2</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Feature wall 12 ft wide</td><td>3</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Feature wall 14 ft wide</td><td>3</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Room 10 x 10 ft</td><td>8</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>Room 10 x 12 ft</td><td>9</td><td>9</td></tr><tr><td>Room 12 x 12 ft</td><td>10</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>Room 12 x 14 ft</td><td>11</td><td>11</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Notice the difference between the two charts. A 2.4 m wall plus trim is 2.5 m per drop, so a 10.05 m roll gives exactly four drops. An 8 ft wall plus 4 in of trim is about 8 ft 4 in, and four of those need just over 33 ft, so a 33 ft roll gives only three full drops. On 8 ft walls, a pattern repeat often costs you nothing extra.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1536" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-01-feature-wall.jpg" alt="Bedroom with a sage green leaf-pattern wallpaper feature wall behind a linen bed and two white oak bedside tables" class="wp-image-2633" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-01-feature-wall.jpg 1536w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-01-feature-wall-300x200.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-01-feature-wall-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-01-feature-wall-768x512.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1536px) 100vw, 1536px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How the Wallpaper Calculator Works</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It uses the strip method from Bob Vila&#8217;s guide on <a href="https://www.bobvila.com/articles/how-to-measure-for-wallpaper/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">how to measure for wallpaper</a>, instead of dividing the wall area by the roll area. Area alone ignores the paper you waste at the top and bottom of every drop.</p>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Drops needed</strong> = the width to cover (one wall, or the distance all the way around the room) divided by the roll width, rounded up.</li>


<li><strong>Length of each drop</strong> = wall height + 10 cm (4 in) for trimming + the pattern repeat. The trim follows Selected Wallpapers, whose calculator allows <a href="https://www.selectedwallpapers.com/pages/european-rolls-vs-double-rolls" target="_blank" rel="noopener">about 2 in (5 cm) on all sides</a>.</li>


<li><strong>Drops per roll</strong> = roll length divided by the drop length, rounded down. A leftover piece shorter than a full drop is waste.</li>


<li><strong>Rolls</strong> = drops needed divided by drops per roll, rounded up. We add one spare roll on top.</li>
</ol>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The results match the figures in Dunelm&#8217;s <a href="https://dunelm.com/info/buying-guides/how-to-measure-wallpaper" target="_blank" rel="noopener">wallpaper measuring guide</a>: with standard 52 cm x 10.05 m rolls and walls about 2.4 m high, a wallpaper with no repeat gives four drops per roll, and a repeat of up to 64 cm gives three.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Measure for Wallpaper</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Measure the width.</strong> For a feature wall, measure from corner to corner. For a whole room, measure all the way around, including chimney breasts and other projections, as Practical DIY advises.</li>


<li><strong>Measure the height</strong> from the top of the skirting board (baseboard) to the ceiling or coving, in a few places. Use the tallest figure.</li>


<li><strong>Do not take off doors and windows.</strong> Bob Vila&#8217;s guide says not to deduct them. Practical DIY only subtracts very large windows or areas that will not be papered.</li>


<li><strong>Read the label</strong> for the roll width, roll length and pattern repeat, and enter them under More options if they are not standard.</li>
</ol>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-02-rolls-and-tools.jpg" alt="Four rolls of sage botanical wallpaper on an oak table with a pasting brush, smoothing tool, tape measure and pencil" class="wp-image-2635" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-02-rolls-and-tools.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-02-rolls-and-tools-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-02-rolls-and-tools-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-02-rolls-and-tools-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Pattern Repeat Explained</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The pattern repeat is the height after which the design starts again. To line the pattern up across the seams, each drop has to start at the same point in the design, so you lose up to one repeat of paper on every drop. Practical DIY notes that, generally, <a href="https://www.practicaldiy.com/decorating/wallpaper/wallpaper_quantity.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the larger the vertical repeat, the more rolls you will need</a>, because the waste is greater.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Plain papers, textured papers and designs labeled free match have no repeat to line up. Even a small repeat of around 27 cm (10.6 in) can cost you a whole drop per roll on 2.4 m walls, and large repeats of 53 or 64 cm (21 to 25 in) waste more on taller walls. On a big room, that adds several rolls.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Wallpaper Roll Sizes</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Roll</th><th>Width</th><th>Length</th><th>Covers about</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Standard UK roll</td><td>52 cm (20.5 in)</td><td>10.05 m (33 ft)</td><td>5.2 m² (56 sq ft)</td></tr><tr><td>European roll</td><td>53 cm (20.9 in)</td><td>10 m (32.8 ft)</td><td>5.3 m² (57 sq ft)</td></tr><tr><td>US double roll</td><td>20.5 to 27 in</td><td>27 to 33 ft</td><td>56 to 70 sq ft</td></tr><tr><td>US single roll</td><td>20.5 to 27 in</td><td>13.5 to 16.5 ft</td><td>28 to 35 sq ft</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Sizes come from Practical DIY (standard UK roll) and <a href="https://www.selectedwallpapers.com/pages/european-rolls-vs-double-rolls" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Selected Wallpapers</a> (European and US rolls). Selected Wallpapers notes that one European roll is roughly one American double roll. Before you order, check whether the price and the label refer to a single or a double roll.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-03-papered-room.jpg" alt="Living room papered all around in a small terracotta and cream pattern, neatly cut around a white door and a window" class="wp-image-2636" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-03-papered-room.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-03-papered-room-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-03-papered-room-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/wallpaper-calculator-03-papered-room-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Before You Buy</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Buy one spare roll</strong>, or two for a large repeat on a big room. Bob Vila suggests one or two more rolls than you think you need, and Practical DIY adds a couple for large repeats.</li>


<li><strong>Check the batch number</strong> on every roll. Practical DIY advises buying only rolls with the same batch or lot number to ensure color matching, and Dunelm recommends buying extra for complex rooms to ensure batch continuity.</li>


<li><strong>Keep the spare</strong> with its label. It is useful for repairs later.</li>
</ul>



